WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:07.565
Our next speaker
is Susan Holmes,

00:00:07.566 --> 00:00:10.890
It's a great pleasure
to have her today.

00:00:10.891 --> 00:00:14.400
She's a professor at Stanford,
she's very interested in

00:00:14.401 --> 00:00:18.020
developing new computational and
statistical approaches to

00:00:18.021 --> 00:00:20.890
a rather complex
biological data sets.

00:00:20.891 --> 00:00:24.017
And today, she's going to tell
us about challenges posed by

00:00:24.018 --> 00:00:26.183
genealogy of human microbiome. Thanks.

00:00:26.184 --> 00:00:27.970
&gt;&gt; Okay, thank you.

00:00:27.971 --> 00:00:30.543
Thank you for inviting me.

00:00:30.544 --> 00:00:33.597
Only invite me to
Boston in October,

00:00:33.598 --> 00:00:35.800
that's all I have to say.

00:00:35.801 --> 00:00:37.150
I lived here for three years.

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:42.790
Okay, so I got really
interested in microbiome about

00:00:42.791 --> 00:00:44.780
more than 10 years ago.

00:00:44.781 --> 00:00:47.314
And the challenge here is,
multi domain data.

00:00:47.315 --> 00:00:50.642
And that's really what
I'm interested in,

00:00:50.643 --> 00:00:52.838
that is heterogeneous data.

00:00:52.839 --> 00:00:56.917
Are challenge of everything that
we're doing today in biology,

00:00:56.918 --> 00:01:01.120
that is we're trying to combine
not just one source of data.

00:01:01.121 --> 00:01:03.950
People talk about big data,
I'm not interested in that.

00:01:03.951 --> 00:01:07.280
I think that the multi
domain is the challenge.

00:01:07.281 --> 00:01:11.228
And the data I'm gonna talk
to you about today comes from

00:01:11.229 --> 00:01:14.171
several projects that
I'm involved in.

00:01:14.172 --> 00:01:18.134
[COUGH[ I have a large grant
with David Relman to study

00:01:18.135 --> 00:01:22.519
resilience an perturbation in
the human microbiome and I also

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:26.410
work on the March of Dimes
study of preterm pregnancy.

00:01:26.411 --> 00:01:31.326
And so we're trying to look at
perturbations on longitudinal

00:01:31.327 --> 00:01:32.740
data.

00:01:32.741 --> 00:01:34.840
And I'll explain a little
bit some of the challenge.

00:01:34.841 --> 00:01:39.190
A lot of them have to do with
the quality of the different

00:01:39.191 --> 00:01:43.824
types of data that come in,
the different types of noise,

00:01:43.825 --> 00:01:46.487
the graph and tree integration.

00:01:46.488 --> 00:01:50.980
And elements that usually
you couldn't just put in

00:01:50.981 --> 00:01:55.152
a Euclidean distance
in a standard way.

00:01:55.153 --> 00:01:58.170
And then, I'm very wedded
to reproduce ability, so

00:01:58.171 --> 00:02:02.920
all of my code, and all the data
I'm gonna talk about that's been

00:02:02.921 --> 00:02:06.020
published which is already
publicly available.

00:02:06.021 --> 00:02:07.150
So what are the data.

00:02:07.151 --> 00:02:10.810
So happily, we already had
the talk at lunchtime about

00:02:10.811 --> 00:02:16.500
the human microbiome and the
biota, and so I want to talk.

00:02:16.501 --> 00:02:19.440
I agree completely that it's
the place where medicine meets

00:02:19.441 --> 00:02:20.090
ecology.

00:02:20.091 --> 00:02:24.070
I think that's
an interesting explanation.

00:02:24.071 --> 00:02:27.470
And the different types
of data that we have come

00:02:27.471 --> 00:02:28.950
at different levels.

00:02:28.951 --> 00:02:32.990
There's one gene which is
called the 16S rRNA gene,

00:02:32.991 --> 00:02:35.540
which serves as a signature
fingerprint for

00:02:35.541 --> 00:02:38.700
all the different
species of bacteria.

00:02:38.701 --> 00:02:41.880
And so that's one which
we concentrate a lot on.

00:02:41.881 --> 00:02:44.670
There's also the fact that
you can just shotgun and

00:02:44.671 --> 00:02:48.110
sequence everything that's there
and look at all the genes.

00:02:48.111 --> 00:02:49.760
And that's called
the metagenome.

00:02:49.761 --> 00:02:52.770
And you can do what we do
on other types of data,

00:02:52.771 --> 00:02:56.877
RNA seq to see what's expressed
by all these different genes.

00:02:56.878 --> 00:03:00.300
And we do a lot of mass spec as
well to look at the proteomics.

00:03:00.301 --> 00:03:03.410
And then there's a lot of
clinical variables and

00:03:03.411 --> 00:03:06.960
environmental variables which
are really important like diet

00:03:06.961 --> 00:03:11.440
and time, seasonal effects and
things like that.

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:14.990
We have a lot of domain
knowledge that you can't

00:03:14.991 --> 00:03:15.770
just ignore.

00:03:15.771 --> 00:03:20.060
So some people feel as if
in order to be objective,

00:03:20.061 --> 00:03:21.806
you have to be dumb.

00:03:21.807 --> 00:03:23.891
And so you have to pretend
that you only know one

00:03:23.892 --> 00:03:25.070
thing at a time.

00:03:25.071 --> 00:03:27.370
And that is just a big mistake,
so

00:03:27.371 --> 00:03:30.030
you're throwing out
a lot of information.

00:03:30.031 --> 00:03:33.980
And you want to take into
account everything you know

00:03:33.981 --> 00:03:36.210
in the way you
analyze the data and

00:03:36.211 --> 00:03:38.470
that's one of the things
that we really try to do.

00:03:38.471 --> 00:03:41.115
That is integrate networks or

00:03:41.116 --> 00:03:45.710
phylogenetics trees into
the way that we compare things.

00:03:45.711 --> 00:03:49.580
And I like that here there
hasn't been a single talk

00:03:49.581 --> 00:03:52.010
where people have
talked about meta data.

00:03:52.011 --> 00:03:54.600
They talk about phenotypes and
I'm with you.

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:58.840
That is, there's a whole field
of bio informatics who've left,

00:03:58.841 --> 00:04:02.184
who let then and say,
carry on with their metadata.

00:04:02.185 --> 00:04:03.550
And this isn't really data.

00:04:03.551 --> 00:04:07.600
Everything you collect is data
and you have to integrate it.

00:04:07.601 --> 00:04:08.682
So we have to find out how

00:04:08.683 --> 00:04:10.531
to combine the data
of different types.

00:04:10.532 --> 00:04:13.744
We've written a computational
system around our

00:04:13.745 --> 00:04:17.874
own bio conductor which makes
heterogeneous data objects where

00:04:17.875 --> 00:04:20.240
you don't lose any of the links.

00:04:20.241 --> 00:04:24.520
Between the clinical variables,
the phylogenetic tree, the taxa,

00:04:24.521 --> 00:04:29.010
the samples across time, and
that's been very important.

00:04:29.011 --> 00:04:32.064
From a more mathematical
point of view and

00:04:32.065 --> 00:04:35.729
since we're trying to
look at the computations,

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:40.108
I think it's useful to think
about many of these data sets.

00:04:40.109 --> 00:04:43.560
Although it's not economical for
graphs for instance but

00:04:43.561 --> 00:04:45.400
think about them as matrices.

00:04:45.401 --> 00:04:47.590
So you have many matrices,

00:04:47.591 --> 00:04:52.560
you might have time series
the same subjects overtime,

00:04:52.561 --> 00:04:55.950
so you can consider the most
matrices of counts.

00:04:55.951 --> 00:04:58.708
You might have
posterior distribution,

00:04:58.709 --> 00:05:01.079
there's many matrices overtime.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:03.809
Networks and longitudinal
studies they're still matrices,

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:07.010
it's a good way of thinking
about them mathematically.

00:05:07.011 --> 00:05:09.140
And this data is always finite,

00:05:09.141 --> 00:05:13.360
so we don't have to do too
much infinite matrices.

00:05:13.361 --> 00:05:16.900
Now I brought up in
a French dogma, and

00:05:16.901 --> 00:05:20.370
so I'm gonna make you
support it for a few minutes.

00:05:20.371 --> 00:05:23.220
I don't think like
a probabilistic maths

00:05:23.221 --> 00:05:26.370
statistician, I'm
a data analysis and

00:05:26.371 --> 00:05:29.080
I think about everything
geometrically.

00:05:29.081 --> 00:05:31.920
So for me,
we have two jewel spaces.

00:05:31.921 --> 00:05:34.888
You have the space
of the variable say,

00:05:34.889 --> 00:05:40.060
abundances of taxa and all
the samples lie in that space.

00:05:40.061 --> 00:05:43.950
And then you have the other side
of things where you can look at

00:05:43.951 --> 00:05:50.020
the variables, as measured for
each of the different samples.

00:05:50.021 --> 00:05:52.440
Now that duality is
actually useful.

00:05:52.441 --> 00:05:56.110
We create inner products
on both spaces and

00:05:56.111 --> 00:05:59.320
you just decide which
space you care about and

00:05:59.321 --> 00:06:02.110
often it's by cheating
on the dimension.

00:06:02.111 --> 00:06:04.640
That is you look at
the dimension which is very

00:06:04.641 --> 00:06:05.750
large and

00:06:05.751 --> 00:06:10.450
you're going to that's not the
dimensionality of your problem.

00:06:10.451 --> 00:06:14.944
If you only have 50 samples the
dimensionality can't be bigger

00:06:14.945 --> 00:06:15.767
than 50.

00:06:15.768 --> 00:06:18.666
So when people say,
we have 40,000 variables,

00:06:18.667 --> 00:06:22.194
yeah but it's still six and 50
dimensions, so you don't have to

00:06:22.195 --> 00:06:25.104
worry about having so
many things you're measuring.

00:06:25.105 --> 00:06:29.820
It just depends on what
projection you make.

00:06:29.821 --> 00:06:31.810
But if you use any kind
of spectral method or

00:06:31.811 --> 00:06:34.650
kernel methods,
that actually matters.

00:06:34.651 --> 00:06:37.353
It brings down
the dimensionality.

00:06:37.354 --> 00:06:40.523
So we have a geometrical
approach to that and

00:06:40.524 --> 00:06:42.407
we can put metrics on, and

00:06:42.408 --> 00:06:46.101
this really matters when
you do data integration.

00:06:46.102 --> 00:06:48.382
You have to care about
how information,

00:06:48.383 --> 00:06:52.139
how much waiting you're putting
on all the different variables,

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:54.839
cuz they're going to
have a different effect.

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:57.902
And if you feel as if
you know in one domain,

00:06:57.903 --> 00:07:01.907
you have much more information
then in another domain,

00:07:01.908 --> 00:07:04.760
you want to take
that into account.

00:07:04.761 --> 00:07:09.000
This is a little bit similar
to if you have high variance,

00:07:09.001 --> 00:07:12.360
high variability,
on one variable,

00:07:12.361 --> 00:07:16.109
you may not want to make exactly
all the variances be one.

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:22.118
So the rescaling of the tables
and rescaling of the variables,

00:07:22.119 --> 00:07:24.491
they are a little bit similar,

00:07:24.492 --> 00:07:27.152
it's just in spades
in some ways.

00:07:27.153 --> 00:07:30.843
So the standard methods and
many people who've talked here.

00:07:30.844 --> 00:07:34.270
We already talked about PCA and
dimension reduction.

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:39.434
They're all based on,
you've taken in a product,

00:07:39.435 --> 00:07:43.643
you take the eigendecomposition
of the cross product matrix.

00:07:43.644 --> 00:07:47.707
And then you take a couple
of eigen functions and

00:07:47.708 --> 00:07:50.480
you project the data down.

00:07:50.481 --> 00:07:53.948
Now you might do that
in different ways, but

00:07:53.949 --> 00:07:57.151
basically that's
a simple framework.

00:07:57.152 --> 00:08:00.210
And so
I call that generalized PCA, but

00:08:00.211 --> 00:08:04.672
this is very standard and
very easy to do on the computer.

00:08:04.673 --> 00:08:08.858
And what we're going to try and
do is change a little bit

00:08:08.859 --> 00:08:13.140
the metrics that we put in,
the s and the v that are here.

00:08:13.141 --> 00:08:15.281
And we're going
to change them so

00:08:15.282 --> 00:08:17.957
that we integrate more
information about

00:08:17.958 --> 00:08:22.050
the relationships between
the variables that we get.

00:08:22.051 --> 00:08:25.730
So this is the duality
diagram we call.

00:08:25.731 --> 00:08:27.400
And this is why you
can go back and

00:08:27.401 --> 00:08:31.420
forth, as I was saying before,
between which of

00:08:31.421 --> 00:08:36.400
the operators you diagonalize,
and it doesn't.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:41.890
Just to say that you can frame
many of the standard statistical

00:08:41.891 --> 00:08:44.240
methods in this same way.

00:08:44.241 --> 00:08:49.400
So one of the interesting ones,
and one which we'll extra plate.

00:08:49.401 --> 00:08:52.600
In some sense,
it is discriminant analysis.

00:08:52.601 --> 00:08:57.370
So discriminant analysis isn't
the same as PCA just in the fact

00:08:57.371 --> 00:08:59.720
that you don't look for
the directions,

00:08:59.721 --> 00:09:03.490
the eigenvectors that
maximize the simple variance.

00:09:03.491 --> 00:09:06.810
You want to maximize the
variance between the groups and

00:09:06.811 --> 00:09:10.940
you're given the group
variability.

00:09:10.941 --> 00:09:13.520
And usually you're given
labels for the groups, and

00:09:13.521 --> 00:09:16.580
you just want to try and
spread out those labels.

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:21.920
So you can actually write down
the discriminant analysis and

00:09:21.921 --> 00:09:22.825
that's useful.

00:09:22.826 --> 00:09:26.965
In some way as
a decomposition with the same

00:09:26.966 --> 00:09:29.065
community of diagram.

00:09:29.066 --> 00:09:32.793
Is just you have a different
metric and you have a metric for

00:09:32.794 --> 00:09:34.924
instance to takes into account,

00:09:34.925 --> 00:09:37.055
here we have a total
variation and

00:09:37.056 --> 00:09:41.475
then you have the winning class
variation which also occurs.

00:09:41.476 --> 00:09:44.015
And then we're going to just
diagonalize the different

00:09:44.016 --> 00:09:47.260
matrix, and we have
a different waiting scheme.

00:09:47.261 --> 00:09:51.100
But you can think of
discriminant analysis

00:09:51.101 --> 00:09:52.800
as a special case of PCA.

00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:57.830
I want to do something
a little bit different.

00:09:57.831 --> 00:10:03.540
I want to not completely take
perfect groups into account.

00:10:03.541 --> 00:10:06.600
I have groups that
appear along a tree.

00:10:06.601 --> 00:10:11.205
So I'm going to do something
a little bit more sophisticated. Yep?

00:10:11.206 --> 00:10:13.228
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] Analysis or

00:10:13.229 --> 00:10:15.252
the other way around?

00:10:15.253 --> 00:10:17.175
&gt;&gt; Yes, the other way around.

00:10:17.176 --> 00:10:21.582
So discriminant analysis is a
special case of generalized PCA.

00:10:23.594 --> 00:10:28.387
Discriminant analysis is
eigen decomposition and

00:10:28.388 --> 00:10:31.290
you can put in the weight here.

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:33.960
This triplet here.

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:37.620
So that this is a PCA but

00:10:37.621 --> 00:10:40.210
it gives you
the discriminant variables.

00:10:40.211 --> 00:10:42.190
And if you could think
about it this way.

00:10:42.191 --> 00:10:44.090
Think that you're giving
special weights and

00:10:44.091 --> 00:10:47.860
you take the mean points of
each of the groups and you do

00:10:47.861 --> 00:10:51.370
PCA with regards to that and
you have a special weighting.

00:10:51.371 --> 00:10:53.700
And it's, that's the same thing.

00:10:53.701 --> 00:10:57.470
But it's useful when you want
to go to sort of intermediary.

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:02.430
So as I said, we're going to
look at many matrices overtime

00:11:02.431 --> 00:11:04.850
and here I've put in here,

00:11:04.851 --> 00:11:07.250
some of the information that
we'd like to take into account.

00:11:07.251 --> 00:11:10.801
So for the taxa, the different
species we know phylogenetic

00:11:10.802 --> 00:11:12.479
trees these are given to us.

00:11:13.660 --> 00:11:17.800
For genetic networks,
we have genetic interactions,

00:11:17.801 --> 00:11:21.640
we have protein-protein
interactions or genetic

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:28.100
reactions that we can induce and
we already know.

00:11:28.101 --> 00:11:31.560
And when you're doing functional
data, for instance, if you have

00:11:31.561 --> 00:11:35.430
mass spec metabolite data,
you know the metabolic network.

00:11:35.431 --> 00:11:38.340
So we actually want to use
metrics which take all

00:11:38.341 --> 00:11:39.410
of those into account.

00:11:40.700 --> 00:11:44.210
The earliest way
of doing this was

00:11:45.810 --> 00:11:48.620
to say everything
is distances and

00:11:48.621 --> 00:11:53.370
if we can make the right
distance, then we can make

00:11:53.371 --> 00:11:56.970
low dimensional representation
of these distances.

00:11:56.971 --> 00:11:59.830
And this actually came from
the work of Schoenberg.

00:11:59.831 --> 00:12:02.340
And the idea there is to say,

00:12:02.341 --> 00:12:05.790
you're given a matrix it's
symmetric positive numbers.

00:12:05.791 --> 00:12:10.680
We're going to find the points
in Euclidean space which have

00:12:10.681 --> 00:12:13.930
distances which are as
close as possible, so

00:12:13.931 --> 00:12:15.940
those given distances.

00:12:15.941 --> 00:12:20.757
And so that method is called
multi dimensional scaling in one

00:12:20.758 --> 00:12:24.637
world it's called PCoA
in the world of ecology.

00:12:24.638 --> 00:12:29.500
And that's been the workhorse
of most of the early work

00:12:29.501 --> 00:12:33.990
on microbial ecology
is based on this PCoA.

00:12:33.991 --> 00:12:37.770
Now the secret sauce here is
the distance that is you have

00:12:37.771 --> 00:12:41.280
to choose a distance which
is relevant to your data.

00:12:41.281 --> 00:12:45.790
So people use Bray Curtis
distance, there are actually 40

00:12:45.791 --> 00:12:48.290
different distances from
which to choose from.

00:12:48.291 --> 00:12:50.670
So it's quite troubling
because in some sense,

00:12:50.671 --> 00:12:52.720
you already have to know
what you're looking for,

00:12:52.721 --> 00:12:57.660
there's some metric learning
inference in there.

00:12:57.661 --> 00:13:00.770
When you are choosing
your distance.

00:13:00.771 --> 00:13:05.490
But there is a way to take into
account the distance on the tree

00:13:05.491 --> 00:13:12.690
between the species and then
doing a PCoA on those points.

00:13:12.691 --> 00:13:14.250
So the way that works and

00:13:14.251 --> 00:13:16.920
this is called double principle
coordinate analysis and

00:13:16.921 --> 00:13:22.090
that was first developed by
ecologist and my PhD student,

00:13:22.091 --> 00:13:24.950
Elizabeth Purdum,
developed it for the microbiom.

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:29.860
And that's that you're
given the samples are at

00:13:29.861 --> 00:13:34.550
different locations say,
and those

00:13:34.551 --> 00:13:39.100
locations you have measurements
of abundances of all the taxa.

00:13:39.101 --> 00:13:43.630
And you have a tree
relationship between the taxa.

00:13:43.631 --> 00:13:48.280
So you start taking the distance
on the tree between the species.

00:13:48.281 --> 00:13:52.930
You build a coordinate space,
Euclidean.

00:13:52.931 --> 00:13:54.800
And then you take the points,

00:13:54.801 --> 00:13:58.590
the abundances as the weights
on the coordinate spaces.

00:13:58.591 --> 00:14:00.490
And so
that's a little bit abstract,

00:14:00.491 --> 00:14:03.140
let me show you on an example
but that's the idea,

00:14:03.141 --> 00:14:06.190
it's called double principle
coordinate analysis.

00:14:06.191 --> 00:14:09.810
So this is what it looks like
on antibiotic time course data.

00:14:09.811 --> 00:14:13.550
So in this first preliminary
study that David Relman did.

00:14:14.590 --> 00:14:16.304
There were three subjects and

00:14:16.305 --> 00:14:21.000
there were about
2,500 different taxa

00:14:21.001 --> 00:14:25.710
that were measured on 50 time
points on these three subjects.

00:14:25.711 --> 00:14:29.790
We're trying to understand
the time points corresponded.

00:14:29.791 --> 00:14:32.740
There were two courses
of antibiotics.

00:14:32.741 --> 00:14:34.450
I'm trying to understand
the resilience,

00:14:34.451 --> 00:14:36.960
that is some of the subjects

00:14:36.961 --> 00:14:42.450
change under antibiotic stress
the abundances of the bacteria.

00:14:42.451 --> 00:14:47.080
And so the way that I just said,
we take the or

00:14:47.081 --> 00:14:51.590
the relationship between the
different taxa which is known.

00:14:51.591 --> 00:14:55.650
And you can make
a multidimensional scaling,

00:14:55.651 --> 00:15:00.260
a PCoA of those points and then
take each of these points and

00:15:00.261 --> 00:15:04.470
their abundances, and
take those as the weights.

00:15:04.471 --> 00:15:06.857
And then you get
a representation of all

00:15:06.858 --> 00:15:08.270
the samples.

00:15:08.271 --> 00:15:12.302
So the first thing you see here
is the green, the red, and

00:15:12.303 --> 00:15:13.060
the blue.

00:15:13.061 --> 00:15:15.942
The colors are all
the different subjects.

00:15:15.943 --> 00:15:19.543
The best things about
the microbiome is the only thing

00:15:19.544 --> 00:15:23.875
that matters if you're trying
to differentiate is the person.

00:15:23.876 --> 00:15:27.160
Every person has their own
ecology, there are lots of

00:15:27.161 --> 00:15:30.300
degrees of freedom and
we're all very different.

00:15:30.301 --> 00:15:33.930
So, that is the main motivation
why all the studies I'm going to

00:15:33.931 --> 00:15:35.680
show you are longitudinal,

00:15:35.681 --> 00:15:39.840
that is you can only really
use the personal as their own

00:15:39.841 --> 00:15:43.100
control when you're doing
a perturbation study.

00:15:43.101 --> 00:15:47.660
And this is the relationship
that we see, now what I want to

00:15:47.661 --> 00:15:50.610
show you really is what
happens with the perturbation.

00:15:50.611 --> 00:15:53.650
So under stress, I'm gonna make
some of those samples be of

00:15:53.651 --> 00:15:55.930
one type,
those would be the stress,

00:15:55.931 --> 00:15:59.080
that is when they're taking
antibiotics and just after.

00:16:00.260 --> 00:16:04.760
And the other will be
what happened before and

00:16:04.761 --> 00:16:06.960
a long time after
the antibiotics.

00:16:06.961 --> 00:16:14.200
So the E1 is under stress and E2
is when they're back to normal.

00:16:14.201 --> 00:16:17.289
So what you see is
within each subject,

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:20.481
so the subject to
subject differences.

00:16:20.482 --> 00:16:22.499
But there is a tendency for

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:26.170
these two subjects to
have the same direction,

00:16:26.171 --> 00:16:30.957
they shifted in the same way so
the stress seems to be the same.

00:16:30.958 --> 00:16:36.038
Here we had a particular
type of subject who was.

00:16:36.039 --> 00:16:37.205
Didn't react.

00:16:37.206 --> 00:16:40.424
So wasn't resilient
in the same way.

00:16:40.425 --> 00:16:44.010
And so, these representations
enabled us to see, well,

00:16:44.011 --> 00:16:48.340
there's something to be studied
about what they do together,

00:16:48.341 --> 00:16:50.640
what is common to the subjects.

00:16:50.641 --> 00:16:53.870
And some of the subjects,
one group of subjects sort of

00:16:53.871 --> 00:16:55.570
don't seem to show
much difference.

00:16:57.240 --> 00:17:01.690
So we did a larger study, and
I'm gonna show you that now.

00:17:01.691 --> 00:17:04.310
And this study just appeared

00:17:04.311 --> 00:17:07.890
in August in
Plus Computational Biology.

00:17:07.891 --> 00:17:11.260
And here what we
wanted to do was adapt

00:17:11.261 --> 00:17:14.940
the DPCoA to be less severe.

00:17:14.941 --> 00:17:20.900
That is, in some sense, we don't
believe that the only component

00:17:20.901 --> 00:17:24.400
variants, the only thing
that's moving the variability,

00:17:24.401 --> 00:17:26.805
is the phylogenetic tree.

00:17:26.806 --> 00:17:30.045
We believe that evolution and

00:17:30.046 --> 00:17:32.835
the tree between the different
taxa is important.

00:17:32.836 --> 00:17:36.150
But we believe there's a whole
part of the variability which

00:17:36.151 --> 00:17:37.649
isn't linked to the tree.

00:17:37.650 --> 00:17:39.980
So we wanted to
modulate the tree.

00:17:39.981 --> 00:17:44.356
And so this is a sparkline plot.

00:17:44.357 --> 00:17:48.115
In fact, every line that's
coming out of this is a time

00:17:48.116 --> 00:17:51.540
series, and I'll give
you a better plot of it.

00:17:51.541 --> 00:17:55.215
But this is the phylogenetic
tree of the different taxa that

00:17:55.216 --> 00:17:55.980
are found.

00:17:55.981 --> 00:18:02.080
So first of all, this is
a small part of a larger study.

00:18:02.081 --> 00:18:04.890
Here, we only have
eight patients.

00:18:04.891 --> 00:18:08.650
We actually have
100 subjects that

00:18:08.651 --> 00:18:11.790
we recruited through
the Quantified Self.

00:18:11.791 --> 00:18:14.510
So wonderful method for
recruiting subjects,

00:18:14.511 --> 00:18:16.210
the Quantified Self.

00:18:16.211 --> 00:18:21.526
Big problem,
not a good demographic sample,

00:18:21.527 --> 00:18:24.123
the Quantified Self.

00:18:24.124 --> 00:18:27.215
It's young men between
the age of 25 and 35.

00:18:27.216 --> 00:18:30.095
We had more than 100
people of these type sign

00:18:30.096 --> 00:18:31.681
up within the first week.

00:18:31.682 --> 00:18:34.870
And then we had to say, no, no,
we don't want any more of you.

00:18:34.871 --> 00:18:39.934
[LAUGH] Because usually
recruitment is a big problem.

00:18:39.935 --> 00:18:42.556
So we were very excited to
begin with all these people

00:18:42.557 --> 00:18:43.278
recruiting.

00:18:43.279 --> 00:18:47.090
Then we look at the profiles and
they're completely homogeneous.

00:18:47.091 --> 00:18:51.636
[LAUGH] So
this is a subset of them.

00:18:51.637 --> 00:18:56.067
And these canonically
announce that the subject

00:18:56.068 --> 00:18:57.909
volunteered to do.

00:18:57.910 --> 00:18:59.915
And they were, as you see,

00:18:59.916 --> 00:19:03.333
the time points are not
equally distributed.

00:19:03.334 --> 00:19:06.283
That is,
we have very heavy density in

00:19:06.284 --> 00:19:09.150
sampling around
the perturbation.

00:19:09.151 --> 00:19:12.380
So that's a carefully
designed statistical study.

00:19:12.381 --> 00:19:14.510
In some sense, that is,
in statistics,

00:19:14.511 --> 00:19:17.740
when you make an experimental
design, you always collect

00:19:17.741 --> 00:19:22.410
more points where you have high
variance, so high perturbation.

00:19:22.411 --> 00:19:27.214
And then outside we have more
regularly sampled points.

00:19:27.215 --> 00:19:29.690
And this is not antibiotics.

00:19:29.691 --> 00:19:32.133
We have several runs
of perturbations for

00:19:32.134 --> 00:19:33.328
the same subjects.

00:19:33.329 --> 00:19:36.230
This is just a canonic T-null.

00:19:36.231 --> 00:19:39.258
So here we actually,
mathematically,

00:19:39.259 --> 00:19:45.090
constructed a prior which
was in between the tree

00:19:47.120 --> 00:19:51.570
and just having an ordinary
variance/covariance matrix.

00:19:51.571 --> 00:19:55.230
And the way we did it is
by constructing a kernal.

00:19:55.231 --> 00:19:59.290
And in order to make a kernel
which sorta obeys a tree,

00:19:59.291 --> 00:20:01.000
we took Brownian motion.

00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:07.090
And if the correlation
is very high,

00:20:07.091 --> 00:20:09.310
we suppose that along
the Brownian motion,

00:20:09.311 --> 00:20:12.780
the particles sustain
the same along the same path.

00:20:12.781 --> 00:20:15.125
And then when they're
splitting off,

00:20:15.126 --> 00:20:17.053
then they become independent.

00:20:17.054 --> 00:20:18.920
They're losing their dependency.

00:20:18.921 --> 00:20:22.940
So the length of the branches
high up in the tree

00:20:22.941 --> 00:20:25.810
tells you how correlated
the variables are.

00:20:25.811 --> 00:20:29.470
So this is quite a realistic
model for a phylogenetic tree

00:20:29.471 --> 00:20:33.230
but it enables us to build,
in some sense, this kernel.

00:20:33.231 --> 00:20:34.629
And I won't [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:34.630 --> 00:20:35.237
Yes?

00:20:35.238 --> 00:20:38.243
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] structure?

00:20:38.244 --> 00:20:43.358
&gt;&gt; So the nodes here are going
to be the taxa, yep.

00:20:43.359 --> 00:20:47.685
And so we're going to build a
metric such that the covariance,

00:20:47.686 --> 00:20:51.685
in some sense, is, if the taxa
are right down to the bottom

00:20:51.686 --> 00:20:55.950
of the tree together, they're
going to be very correlated.

00:20:57.760 --> 00:20:58.453
But that's the idea.

00:20:58.454 --> 00:21:01.570
But we actually,
how can I say it?

00:21:01.571 --> 00:21:07.077
We put a weight on the part
of which was the ordinary

00:21:07.078 --> 00:21:12.595
variance and the part which
is actually the tree.

00:21:12.596 --> 00:21:13.923
So you can modulate.

00:21:13.924 --> 00:21:18.003
This is like a one
parameter family that goes

00:21:18.004 --> 00:21:22.580
from ordinary PCA all
the way to DPCoA.

00:21:22.581 --> 00:21:26.200
So you can modulate, and you can
actually get the data to tell

00:21:26.201 --> 00:21:30.420
you what the ideal
tuning parameter is.

00:21:30.421 --> 00:21:34.550
And this is called
adaptive generalized PCA.

00:21:34.551 --> 00:21:36.640
And it's Julia Fukiyama
who wrote,

00:21:36.641 --> 00:21:39.370
there's a program on
GitHub that you can use.

00:21:39.371 --> 00:21:41.069
And, yes?

00:21:41.070 --> 00:21:43.010
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] tell you with
respect to what objective?

00:21:44.180 --> 00:21:46.740
&gt;&gt; Sorry, would it be a metric?

00:21:46.741 --> 00:21:49.310
&gt;&gt; No, with what objective,
when you say let the data tell

00:21:49.311 --> 00:21:52.770
you where to interpolate
between those two?

00:21:52.771 --> 00:21:58.600
&gt;&gt; Well, you can look at
the different criteria.

00:21:58.601 --> 00:22:01.910
You can do it by
cross-validation or you can do

00:22:01.911 --> 00:22:05.400
it with regards to some other
information that you have.

00:22:05.401 --> 00:22:08.560
In this case, we actually have
a change point which we know.

00:22:08.561 --> 00:22:11.304
As I say, I'm not
an objective statistician.

00:22:11.305 --> 00:22:13.290
I refuse to be dumb.

00:22:13.291 --> 00:22:15.910
So I know something
about this data.

00:22:15.911 --> 00:22:21.510
I know when we gave
the cleanout to the subjects.

00:22:21.511 --> 00:22:26.390
So if we can find a change point
which is coherent with our data,

00:22:26.391 --> 00:22:28.420
that's what I'm looking for.

00:22:28.421 --> 00:22:30.570
And this is the output.

00:22:30.571 --> 00:22:34.160
So here's the phylogenetic
tree of the different taxa.

00:22:34.161 --> 00:22:38.010
These are coefficients which
actually correspond to the taxa,

00:22:38.011 --> 00:22:39.410
and they appear on the branches.

00:22:39.411 --> 00:22:41.970
It's a little bit
hard to see here.

00:22:41.971 --> 00:22:47.250
But here is how these different
taxa jump up and down.

00:22:47.251 --> 00:22:52.360
And so, here you can see that
this RSV305 goes way down

00:22:52.361 --> 00:22:57.055
during the perturbation and then
reestablishes itself perfectly,

00:22:57.056 --> 00:22:59.260
so very resilient taxa.

00:22:59.261 --> 00:23:02.710
So we have these profiles
of these different taxa.

00:23:02.711 --> 00:23:06.050
And we could see,
that's what we're interested in.

00:23:06.051 --> 00:23:08.140
So that enables us to see that.

00:23:09.820 --> 00:23:13.560
So I can't give you all
the details in the paper.

00:23:13.561 --> 00:23:19.305
We have a Docker image and
three bioconductor packages

00:23:19.306 --> 00:23:23.085
in the dataset which are all
public, which go with the paper.

00:23:23.086 --> 00:23:26.385
So I encourage you to ask
me questions offline or

00:23:26.386 --> 00:23:29.095
by email if you want
to try this out.

00:23:29.096 --> 00:23:31.770
I do think it's
really important to

00:23:31.771 --> 00:23:34.440
say that everybody could have
come up with a different method.

00:23:34.441 --> 00:23:36.160
We could have done
different types of models.

00:23:36.161 --> 00:23:39.930
And it's interesting to
compare between methods.

00:23:39.931 --> 00:23:42.315
And so I'm open to other
people analyzing the data and

00:23:42.316 --> 00:23:45.023
telling me, you got that
completely wrong, and so on.

00:23:45.024 --> 00:23:50.030
So we do try to make a
reproducible research workflow.

00:23:50.031 --> 00:23:52.670
And you saw these RSVs,
and in fact,

00:23:52.671 --> 00:23:57.415
what they are, I've changed
the names from OTUs.

00:23:57.416 --> 00:24:00.790
So my field,
we often call them OTUs.

00:24:00.791 --> 00:24:06.790
There's been a big mistake
made in defining the unit,

00:24:06.791 --> 00:24:08.950
the strains with which we work.

00:24:08.951 --> 00:24:13.570
And I took OTUs to
be standard and

00:24:13.571 --> 00:24:17.870
I didn't ask how this raw data
was profiled at the beginning.

00:24:17.871 --> 00:24:20.520
And I realized that there
had been a mistake.

00:24:20.521 --> 00:24:23.170
And so I'm gonna talk to you a
little bit about what we did for

00:24:23.171 --> 00:24:24.130
this mistake.

00:24:24.131 --> 00:24:26.902
And this is about data two.

00:24:26.903 --> 00:24:30.686
And so if you want to see
exactly how you use all of this

00:24:30.687 --> 00:24:35.502
data two, we did make a complete
workflow in F1000Research,

00:24:35.503 --> 00:24:37.002
which has the code.

00:24:37.003 --> 00:24:40.823
So the strain-level analysis,
and

00:24:40.824 --> 00:24:46.245
this came up in the talk
that Oleg gave this morning,

00:24:46.246 --> 00:24:51.683
that we'd like to know more
about certain strains.

00:24:51.684 --> 00:24:54.118
And I'll just motivate it and
tell you why.

00:24:54.119 --> 00:24:59.481
We did two studies on pregnancy
and I'm predicting preterm.

00:24:59.482 --> 00:25:04.181
And the main thing that came out
when we did these studies was

00:25:04.182 --> 00:25:08.223
that there are certain taxa
which came out as really

00:25:08.224 --> 00:25:09.460
important.

00:25:09.461 --> 00:25:14.079
And the main one that
came out was Gardnerella.

00:25:14.080 --> 00:25:18.820
And we were able to
revisit the data.

00:25:18.821 --> 00:25:23.540
And actually we took a class
of subjects, so different

00:25:23.541 --> 00:25:28.040
ethnicity, different background,
and we found the same results.

00:25:28.041 --> 00:25:31.660
But we also found that it wasn't
all the strains of gun they're

00:25:31.661 --> 00:25:34.560
out of that were associated
with preterm births.

00:25:34.561 --> 00:25:37.390
So that was that something
that was important for us.

00:25:37.391 --> 00:25:41.920
And that's why it's sort of the
attention to detail in this case

00:25:41.921 --> 00:25:43.270
is very important.

00:25:43.271 --> 00:25:46.200
So here,
there's Gardnerella screening.

00:25:46.201 --> 00:25:50.120
We were able to do it by
ignoring what I said about

00:25:50.121 --> 00:25:50.800
distances.

00:25:50.801 --> 00:25:54.360
So distances, forget all
the beginning of the talk.

00:25:54.361 --> 00:25:56.410
I said distances were
really important.

00:25:56.411 --> 00:25:58.560
Distances aren't everything.

00:25:58.561 --> 00:26:02.180
And this is a mistake that
we made doing OT use.

00:26:02.181 --> 00:26:06.530
So it's much better to
have strain information

00:26:06.531 --> 00:26:08.440
than not have strain
information here.

00:26:08.441 --> 00:26:11.090
When you follow
the different women,

00:26:11.091 --> 00:26:16.590
you can see that the strains
stay about the same.

00:26:16.591 --> 00:26:19.670
But we don't have the strain
resolution when you use the old

00:26:19.671 --> 00:26:20.980
method.

00:26:20.981 --> 00:26:24.930
So how do people build
what are called OTUs?

00:26:24.931 --> 00:26:26.890
They use a distance.

00:26:26.891 --> 00:26:32.070
And so if you have strains which
occur 100 times more than other

00:26:32.071 --> 00:26:38.080
strains, the OTU method
take a radius of

00:26:38.081 --> 00:26:42.450
a fixed distance, which doesn't
take into account the frequency.

00:26:42.451 --> 00:26:43.996
And that's a bad
kind of distance.

00:26:43.997 --> 00:26:48.125
You want a distance which tells
you whether you have, and

00:26:48.126 --> 00:26:51.823
this is what annoys me when
people compare women and

00:26:51.824 --> 00:26:54.970
men in Math,
Larry Summers and so on.

00:26:54.971 --> 00:26:58.350
The thing is, if you don't
have the same number of

00:26:58.351 --> 00:27:03.020
baseline people you're comparing
to, of course if you have 10,

00:27:03.021 --> 00:27:08.470
000 men, you have much
higher extreme points.

00:27:08.471 --> 00:27:09.710
If you have a hundred women,

00:27:09.711 --> 00:27:13.020
you're gonna have much
less extreme points.

00:27:13.021 --> 00:27:17.100
So, the frequency on
the line is not independent.

00:27:18.490 --> 00:27:21.410
The extreme points are not
independent of the frequencies,

00:27:21.411 --> 00:27:24.550
and this is a mistake that
everybody seems to make.

00:27:24.551 --> 00:27:28.469
And so, this was a mistake
that people made on OTUs.

00:27:28.470 --> 00:27:33.621
And we modeled the frequencies,
taking into account that

00:27:33.622 --> 00:27:39.450
some of the OTUs, some of the
strains are much more frequent.

00:27:39.451 --> 00:27:42.260
You're going to see many more
errors if they're much more

00:27:42.261 --> 00:27:43.410
frequent.

00:27:43.411 --> 00:27:45.340
And if you take
that into account,

00:27:45.341 --> 00:27:52.682
we have this error model and
we were able to, sorry.

00:27:52.683 --> 00:27:55.860
I'm gonna go back to show
you what this gave us.

00:27:55.861 --> 00:28:00.380
This comes from this new
probabilistic error model

00:28:00.381 --> 00:28:03.880
in which we've incorporated
the frequencies

00:28:03.881 --> 00:28:05.575
with which you see the strains.

00:28:05.576 --> 00:28:10.399
And we're able to denoise, if
you have a strain which occurs

00:28:10.400 --> 00:28:16.357
a 100,000 times, you're going
to see, with some error,

00:28:16.358 --> 00:28:20.634
five errors away will be
quite frequent within that.

00:28:20.635 --> 00:28:24.578
And if you have a sequence which
is five away, it's probably just

00:28:24.579 --> 00:28:28.170
a noisy version of your
very frequent sequence.

00:28:28.171 --> 00:28:31.140
So this method has completely
changed how we're able to do

00:28:31.141 --> 00:28:32.580
the straining.

00:28:32.581 --> 00:28:35.050
And I wish we could do it for
metagenomics and

00:28:35.051 --> 00:28:37.800
if anybody is interested in
doing a really hard project.

00:28:37.801 --> 00:28:41.230
It would be to take this,
what we've done for one gene and

00:28:41.231 --> 00:28:44.240
do it for
a whole set of marker genes.

00:28:44.241 --> 00:28:48.613
And so that's a paper that
appeared in Nature Methods with

00:28:48.614 --> 00:28:49.780
Ben Callahan.

00:28:49.781 --> 00:28:54.597
And I don't have time to tell
you about the registration that

00:28:54.598 --> 00:28:59.319
we did to do uncertainty, but
I just show you this picture,

00:28:59.320 --> 00:29:02.342
my goal has been that
when we make these

00:29:02.343 --> 00:29:06.780
multidimensional dimension
of plots with PCOA.

00:29:06.781 --> 00:29:10.230
We don't use ellipses,
the data are never normal.

00:29:10.231 --> 00:29:14.780
You can't use ellipses but
you can do Bayesian computations

00:29:14.781 --> 00:29:17.530
which give you Bayesian
confidence intervals.

00:29:17.531 --> 00:29:21.360
And here we have confidence
regions around points by using

00:29:21.361 --> 00:29:25.400
Bayesian posteriors and it's
much better than just trying to

00:29:25.401 --> 00:29:28.590
say everything is normally
distributed which it's not.

00:29:28.591 --> 00:29:31.307
So I'm going to finish.

00:29:31.308 --> 00:29:34.887
Thank all the statisticians who
make all their data available in

00:29:34.888 --> 00:29:36.950
bio conductor because
I use it a lot.

00:29:38.450 --> 00:29:42.280
We have a lot of different
GitHub repos, and

00:29:42.281 --> 00:29:45.060
everything to do with phyloseq
developed by Joey, and

00:29:45.061 --> 00:29:47.710
he has very good tutorials.

00:29:47.711 --> 00:29:49.750
I work mostly with David Relman.

00:29:49.751 --> 00:29:52.610
And Elisabeth Purdom
is the first person I

00:29:52.611 --> 00:29:54.470
